This version stores the smallest value seen for each compressed value rank. While moving from right to left, a query asks for the smallest stored value that is strictly greater than the current value.

The array values can be negative or large, so coordinate compression is used before building the tree. This method is slower than a stack, but it shows another way to search the suffix.

For `[4, 1, 3]`, the value `3` is stored first, then `1` finds `3`, and finally `4` finds no larger stored value.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private const int Missing = int.MaxValue;

    public List<int> nextLargerElement(List<int> arr)
    {
        int n = arr.Count;
        var answer = new List<int>(new int[n]);
        var values = new List<int>(arr);
        values.Sort();

        var unique = new List<int>();
        foreach (int value in values)
        {
            if (unique.Count == 0 || unique[unique.Count - 1] != value)
            {
                unique.Add(value);
            }
        }

        int size = 1;
        while (size < unique.Count)
        {
            size *= 2;
        }

        var tree = new int[size * 2];
        for (int i = 0; i < tree.Length; i++)
        {
            tree[i] = Missing;
        }

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            int rank = unique.BinarySearch(arr[i]);
            int greater = Query(tree, size, rank + 1, unique.Count - 1);
            answer[i] = greater == Missing ? -1 : greater;
            Update(tree, size, rank, arr[i]);
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private void Update(int[] tree, int size, int index, int value)
    {
        int position = size + index;
        tree[position] = Math.Min(tree[position], value);
        position /= 2;

        while (position > 0)
        {
            tree[position] = Math.Min(tree[position * 2], tree[position * 2 + 1]);
            position /= 2;
        }
    }

    private int Query(int[] tree, int size, int left, int right)
    {
        if (left > right)
        {
            return Missing;
        }

        left += size;
        right += size;
        int result = Missing;

        while (left <= right)
        {
            if ((left & 1) == 1)
            {
                result = Math.Min(result, tree[left]);
                left++;
            }

            if ((right & 1) == 0)
            {
                result = Math.Min(result, tree[right]);
                right--;
            }

            left /= 2;
            right /= 2;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` after sorting the values.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the compressed values, tree, and result.

A stack keeps values that are still waiting for their greater value. When the current number is bigger than the stack top, it becomes the answer for every smaller waiting value.

The stack is kept in decreasing order. Each array value enters once and leaves once, so the nested-looking loop is still linear.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> nextLargerElement(vector<int> arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        vector<int> answer(n, -1);
        stack<int> waiting;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (!waiting.empty() && arr[i] > arr[waiting.top()]) {
                answer[waiting.top()] = arr[i];
                waiting.pop();
            }
            waiting.push(i);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Dry run for `[2, 1, 3]`: index `1` waits behind `1`, then `3` resolves both waiting values where needed. The last value has no greater value, so it remains `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and result.

For every position, I look at the values on its right and stop at the first larger one. If the loop finishes without finding one, the answer stays `-1`.

Dry run for `[4, 5, 2, 10]`:
- `4` finds `5`
- `5` finds `10`
- `2` finds `10`
- `10` has no value after it

This is easy to follow, but it checks many pairs when the array is large.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] nextLargerElement(int[] arr) {
        int[] answer = new int[arr.length];

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            answer[i] = -1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[i]) {
                    answer[i] = arr[j];
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the result array.

Instead of scanning from the end, this uses a stack of indices whose answers are not known yet. When a new value is larger, it resolves all smaller pending values before joining the stack.

Equal values are left waiting because the next greater value must be strictly larger. If the input is empty, the returned array is also empty.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public int[] nextLargerElement(int[] arr) {
        int[] answer = new int[arr.length];
        Deque<Integer> pending = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            answer[i] = -1;

            while (!pending.isEmpty()
                    && arr[i] > arr[pending.peek()]) {
                answer[pending.pop()] = arr[i];
            }

            pending.push(i);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

For a decreasing array, no value can resolve a pending index, so all entries correctly stay `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for pending indices.

## Intuition

The main idea is to look at the array from right to left.

For every element, we need the first greater element on its right. We use a stack to keep useful elements from the right side.

If the top of the stack is smaller than or equal to the current element, it cannot be the next greater element, so we remove it.

After removing all such elements:
- If the stack is not empty, its top is the next greater element.
- If the stack is empty, there is no greater element, so the answer remains -1.


## Approach

1. Find the size of the array.
2. If the array is empty, return an empty vector.
3. Create a stack `st` to store possible greater elements.
4. Create an answer vector `ans` initialized with -1.
5. Push the last element into the stack.
6. Traverse the array from right to left.
7. For every element:
   - Remove elements from the stack while they are smaller than or equal to the current element.
   - If the stack is not empty, its top is the next greater element.
   - Store it in `ans[i]`.
   - Push the current element into the stack.
8. Return the answer.

The -1 initialization automatically handles elements that have no greater element on their right, including the last element.


## Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> nextLargerElement(vector<int> arr) {

        int n = arr.size();

        if(n == 0)
            return {};

        stack<int> st;
        vector<int> ans(n, -1);

        st.push(arr[n - 1]);

        for(int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {

            while(!st.empty() && st.top() <= arr[i]) {
                st.pop();
            }

            if(!st.empty()) {
                ans[i] = st.top();
            }

            st.push(arr[i]);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

